  "name": "Cool jazz / West Coast (1950s)",
  "family": "jazz",
  "lineage": "Late-1940s-1950s jazz substyle emerging in response to bebop's frenetic energy, characterized by lighter timbral palette, restrained dynamic register, and arrangement-heavy compositional approach. Miles Davis's Birth of the Cool sessions (1949-1950) established the canonical sound; the West Coast cool subgenre developed in 1950s Los Angeles through Gerry Mulligan, Chet Baker, Lennie Niehaus, Shorty Rogers, and Dave Brubeck. Distinguished from bebop by cool's slower-and-more-relaxed (vs frenetic) emotional register.",
